  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Monday)\nIssued at 1121 AM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nCurrent upper-air analysis and water vapor imagery depict \nsouthwesterly flow and drier air over the region, with the next \nsystem to watch moving into the PNW. Satellite imagery indicates \nclear skies under high pressure. Surface observations show \ntemperatures mainly in the 50s and 60s, with dewpoints in the mid \n30s to mid 40s. Northwesterly winds across the SD plains are light\nat ~10kts, while east-southeasterly winds across northeast WY and\nfar southwest SD are at ~15kts. \n\nDue to the position of the jet stream to our south, today is looking \nto be a nice fall-like day with highs in the upper 60s to mid 70s \nwith lighter winds under clear skies. Fire weather concerns have \nbacked off some today with RH's in the mid to upper 20s to low 30s \nand lighter winds. There will be a chance for light showers and \npossibly a couple thunderstorms late tonight into tomorrow morning \nacross the SD plains, though this will be very spotty with minimal \naccumulations for most. \n\nWednesday looks to be on the warmer side as the jet stream shifts \ntoward the north, with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Breezier \nconditions will also return throughout the day for both western SD \nand northeast WY. The combination of warmer temperatures, breezy \nwinds, and RH values in the low to mid 20s across northeast WY will \nlead to elevated fire conditions. \n\nChances for precipitation will return Thursday/Friday as a shortwave \nmoves across the region as an upper-level low spins over northern \nCA/western OR. Currently, south-central SD has the best chance at \npicking up meaningful precipitation amounts, while far western SD \nand northeast WY may struggle to pick up some desperately needed \nmoisture. Chances for precipitation will continue through the \nweekend as the upper low moves across the region.",
